{"kind":"request","text":"Ship an authoring skill for declared applications with the published package.\n\nShip a skill for writing dap applications with the published package, so an agent that installs dap gets the authoring guidance with the code.\n\nSource: Hugh's request of 28 October 2026, after a review of the alchemy Domain package, which ships a skill with templates inside its package. The roadmap lists an authoring language as not designed, and the dap-spike measured six repairs against a budget of two when an agent wrote one model. A skill is the cheapest answer to that gap that needs no kernel change. The planner may attach this to an existing work item or sequence it.\n\nWanted:\n1. A `skills/dap-application/SKILL.md` with reference files, in the repository and in the `files` of the published CLI package (or the package the planner chooses), installable from the repository by a Claude Code plugin marketplace entry and by Codex's equivalent.\n2. Content derived from docs/write-an-application.md, docs/application-views.md and docs/scopes.md, without duplicating them: the nouns (scope, item, slot, act, guard, effect, send, handler, timed rule, rule expression, capability form); the rules that keep a definition valid (one effect per slot, required slots set at opening, bounded guards and fan-outs, redactable reads, the invariant placement table); templates for an item type, an act, a handler, a timed rule, a rule expression, a declared view and a scenario; the loop (write, `dap check`, read the findings, fix); how to pin, pack and install; what the kernel refuses and why (closed forms, no computed effects, proposals checked by rules).\n3. Naming and layout conventions for an application package, as the grow and lending examples use.\n4. A check that the skill's statements match main: the doc-links check covers its links, and each template is a file that `dap check` validates in CI.","body":{"conditions":"Landed on main with the skill, the marketplace entry, 